{Fischer was fifteen. His opponent, one of the strongest players in the world, lost his queen on move eleven to a knight fork and played on for thirty more moves out of pride.}
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. Be3 Nf6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Bc4 O-O 8. Bb3 Na5 {Going after the bishop, but the knight leaves the centre and the f7 square loses a defender.} 9. e5 Ne8 {The losing move. 9...Nxb3 was necessary.} 10. Bxf7+ {The first blow removes the pawn that shields e6.} Kxf7 11. Ne6 {A fork of the queen on d8 and the bishop on g7. If the pawn takes, the queen goes.} dxe6 12. Qxd8 Nc6 13. Qd2 Bxe5 14. O-O Nd6 15. Bf4 Nc4 16. Qe2 Bxf4 17. Qxc4 Kg7 18. Ne4 Bc7 19. Nc5 Rf6 20. c3 e5 21. Rad1 Nd8 22. Nd7 Rc6 23. Qh4 Re6 24. Nc5 Rf6 25. Ne4 Rf4 26. Qxe7+ Rf7 27. Qa3 Nc6 28. Nd6 Bxd6 29. Rxd6 Bf5 30. b4 Rff8 31. b5 Nd8 32. Rd5 Nf7 33. Rc5 a6 34. b6 Be4 35. Re1 Bc6 36. Rxc6 bxc6 37. b7 Rab8 38. Qxa6 Nd8 39. Rb1 Rf7 40. h3 Rfxb7 41. Rxb7+ Rxb7 42. Qa8 1-0
